## What happened

Handheld mode. In the name-entry keyboard the game draws itself (no `swkbd:` line appears anywhere in the run), tapping a letter highlights it but no character is appended. Keys that also carry a controller mapping (space/Y, delete/B) still respond; the letter grid does not. It happens every time.

Steps: Console mode → Handheld (the Tap touch layout appears) → start the game → open the name entry → tap letters.

`DM_TOUCH_LOG=1` shows a complete Start/held/End sequence at the tapped key, and the coordinates are correct for the key under the finger:

```
touch read sample 974 points 1 | #0 (869,482) attribute 1
touch read sample 975 points 1 | #0 (869,482) attribute 0
touch read sample 976 points 1 | #0 (869,482) attribute 0
touch read sample 977 points 1 | #0 (869,482) attribute 0
touch read sample 978 points 1 | #0 (869,482) attribute 0
touch read sample 979 points 1 | #0 (869,482) attribute 0
touch read sample 980 points 1 | #0 (869,482) attribute 2
```

## What I expected

A tapped letter is entered in the field, as on hardware.

## Suspected cause

`hle_hid.c`'s `touch_read()` calls `touch_fill_n()` at every read, and each read advances the host finger state and consumes the Start/End edges. ACNH reads the touch screen from two places during one game frame (its main input manager, `main+0x1b65260`, and the keyboard's own reader); the first reader takes the Start or End, and the second one only sees a held point. The keyboard therefore highlights the key but never sees a complete tap. On hardware, SDK reads copy one shared HID sample; they do not consume edges.

## Proposed fix

Publish one complete 16-finger sample per guest frame and let every reader copy it, clamped to the requested capacity, with the sampling number shared. Sample on the host's 120 Hz grid before the first present, as the current code effectively does. Console mode keeps its behaviour (empty state, sampling number rising). Gestures are unaffected (`gesture_fill` uses its own finger state).

```c
static uint8_t g_touch_sample[0x290];
static uint64_t g_touch_sample_frame, g_touch_sample_boot;
static int g_touch_sample_valid;
static void touch_read(void *s, int n) {
    keys_sync();
    if (dm_opmode() != 0) { touch_fill_n(s, n, now_ns(), 0); return; }
    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_touch_sample_mu);
    uint64_t frame = dm_guest_frame(), now = now_ns(), boot = frame ? 0 : now / 8333333;
    if (!g_touch_sample_valid || frame != g_touch_sample_frame || boot != g_touch_sample_boot) {
        touch_fill_n(g_touch_sample, 16, now, 1);
        g_touch_sample_frame = frame; g_touch_sample_boot = boot; g_touch_sample_valid = 1;
    }
    memcpy(s, g_touch_sample, 0x10 + 0x28 * (size_t)n);
    if (((int32_t *)s)[2] > n) ((int32_t *)s)[2] = n;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_touch_sample_mu);
}
```

