## Also in the fix's PR: touch points in console mode

The pull request for this issue ([#2603](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/2603)) carries a second, related change to the same function: `DM_TOUCH_DOCKED=1` makes `touch_read()` answer touch points in console mode too.

It is separate from this issue's defect. On the Switch the screen is out of reach when docked, so console mode answers an empty state, and that is correct for hardware. A host whose front end draws into a touchable display (an iPhone, a Mac window) has the picture under the player's finger regardless, so a player who wants the console mode's resolution (1920x1080 through `oe::GetDefaultDisplayResolution`) otherwise loses the touch screen. The switch is off by default, so a game still sees the hardware's empty console state; gestures stay handheld-only, and a game that stops reading the touch screen in console mode itself is unaffected.

Both changes are in `hle_hid.c`'s touch path, which is why they share the pull request; either can be reviewed and taken on its own.

`hidpad_gc_native(vid)` returns 1 for 0x045e, so `hidpad_found` calls `hidpad_keep_gc` for every Microsoft pad without the `HIDPAD_GC_WAIT` from #859. Steam's virtual pad is `GamePad-N`, `045e:028e`, `kIOHIDVirtualHIDevice` = 1, `supportsHIDDevice` = YES, and GameController never lists it (`GCController.controllers()` is empty). So it is kept for GameController and read by nobody; the game log has no `controller ... connected` line.

