### What happened

In adult Zora's Domain, the snow has square holes on an 8×8-pixel grid along its edges, where it meets rock, walls and sky. It happens every time with my test save, and the blocks move from frame to frame. Where: on the snowy path where the save starts, with a signpost on the left, looking toward the frozen domain. The holes show along the snow on both sides of the path.

Cause, as far as I can tell: pixel shader `1d3d97b789b9a6e6` (vertex `5bf3ef25d79ff529`) writes depth while sampling its own pass's depth attachment through a texture slot, to fade its alpha. `06750e798cfec8ef` and `c2352ccb908fa8d0` do the same. That is about 145 draws a frame. Metal leaves that read undefined. On my M3 Max it returns NaN in whole 8×8 blocks, so the alpha becomes 0 and the pixel from before the draw shows through.

A pass dump of the old build (DM_CTL `passdump 1`) shows the hazard whether or not the holes appear on a given Mac. The same texture (serial 53494, format 252 = Depth32Float, 1951×1098) is the pass's depth attachment and the draw's texture in slot 6, and the draw has depth test and depth write on:

```
P 354127 d ser=53494 fmt=252 1951x1098 load
B 354127 st1 tex reg6 tid=12616 real ser=53494 fmt=252 1951x1098x1 ...
D 354127 prim=4 vc=507 ... dt=1 dw=1 df=4 ...
```

A debug copy of the shader (swapped in with `DM_MSL_DIR`) shows the depth read returning NaN in whole 8×8 blocks on the same grid as the holes.

A pass split can't help, because each draw samples what it is writing itself. The existing depth rule already gives each of these draws its own pass: each one samples depth and the next one writes it, so the pass ends between them. The holes are still there. Having these draws sample a depth copy fixes it. The copy is taken at the first such draw since the game's last barrier (in the same submission), then reused by the later ones without ending the pass.

Blocks counted by a tile detector, same save and camera (snow area / rocks area):
- old build: 108 / 37
- with the copy (first version): 5 / 0 (no holes are visible, so these are detector false positives)
- same build, `DM_NVN_FB_DSNAP=0`: 109 / 37
- final version (the three fixes below), two frames: 4 / 0 and 6 / 0

The same build also switched the copy on and off every 600 frames in one run (`DM_NVN_FB_DSNAP=ab DM_AB=600`): about 100 blocks in each off phase, 4-8 in each on phase.

It is also much faster there, because the one-draw passes go away: GPU p50 went from 25 to 15 ms (14.8 ms with the final version), and passes from 244 to 99 a frame. Adult Zora's Domain went from about 39 fps to a steady 60.

One caveat: the later snow draws now see the depth from before the first one, not the snow drawn just before them. NVN only promises the newer depth after a barrier, so either result is allowed. Away from the holes, the fixed snow comes out a little brighter than the old build; I haven't checked yet whether that comes from the reuse or from holes the detector missed.

The patch below is the change ported to main e82888e6. Compared with the version I measured first, it lands a still-pending clear before the copy (`rp_flush`), records a draw's own read marks after its binding loop so the copy's pass end doesn't drop them, and leaves a depth view of another level or layer to the existing rule. `DM_NVN_FB_DSNAP=0` turns it off. It applies to e82888e6 and the host builds (Xcode 27.2), but I have only run it on my tree, not on main. I can open a PR if that helps.

