- The complete original production patch stack and commit graph are no longer available. Selected code was recovered from retained edit records; certainty and fresh checks are distinguished below.

### Gameplay and persistence

- A four-race 50cc Mushroom Cup was completed with four first-place finishes, 60 points, gold/three stars and a character unlock. Sixteen save commits succeeded during that qualification.
- The award, unlock and settings survived normal quit/relaunch.
- A full installed 150cc Whistlestop Summit race completed through daylight and rain.
- The owner subsequently confirmed **the first seven cup-opening movies work**. The eighth cup remained locked and was not checked in gameplay. The owner considers the local work complete.
- Audio, normal window stacking and a Mac launch icon work. The application launched independently after the development tree was moved away, its deep/strict signature verified, and committed save hashes were unchanged across that verification launch.

### Main findings

**Indirect jump-table recognition:** integer-index invalidation used a register-name transformation that could conflate SIMD/vector registers with integer registers. The correction classifies register banks explicitly. The recovered frozen proposal has Git blob `bd7bb59a39fa1d473e1cdddbf165ce58a7ac9999`, exactly matching its historical record; the original pinned source has blob `57902a3957b5c02a853c0b91de63f5d343f98440`. The original real `main` rewrite passed in 66.78 seconds, with exact agreement for the six applicable targets. Historical synthetic/recognizer suites are not gameplay tests.

**Magenta shadows, broken roads and alias coherence:** physical sparse-page aliases needed coherent dirty tracking and retained physical backing for Metal resource lifetime, with readable-span validation. The corrected runs showed solid roads and dark kart shadows. The backing headers, dirty-alias components and fixtures have been reconstructed. The complete original Metal/pool integration patch has not been reassembled. Earlier development kernel panics occurred; their exact kernel trigger was not established, so this is not a claim that it was proven fixed.

**FPS:** repeated full scans of roughly 28,000 dirty-memory ranges dominated texture lookups. A bounded 64-entry thread-local lookup cache retains per-page checks, validates range lifetime and registry membership, and preserves first-containing-range semantics for overlaps. An initial broad-containment cache failed an overlap negative control; the final version limits reusable intervals or retains an exact query when an earlier narrow range overlaps it. A matched race view improved from about 50 ms to 24 ms per frame (about 20 to 41 fps), without reducing image quality.

**Terrain:** sampled uncompressed height/material maps written by the CPU without a flush needed first-use upload and dirty tracking; subsequent GPU ownership stops speculative CPU uploads. This was distinct from the road/shadow issue.

**Cup movies:** decoded VP9 frames and their off-screen conversion were correct, but RGBA8 and RGBA8 sRGB descriptors over the exact same storage were split into separate Metal textures. The compositor sampled the empty sibling. Shared backing with descriptor-specific colour views restores presentation. The recovered correction keeps serial registrations on the shared root and handles usage upgrades through either colour view. This matches the class of issue now documented in the upstream rendering reference.

